gb: point sramBank at the SRAM when the cart has less than one bank of it

Backport of a fix already in mgba-emu/mgba master, which libretro/mgba has not
rebased onto: the bug is present both at our pinned e31759b2 (2026-08-05) and
at libretro/mgba HEAD 7a12d6d4, so bumping MGBA_COMMIT does not remove the need
for this. Drop the patch when a core bump makes it fail to apply -- that is the
signal that libretro has picked the fix up.

MBC2 carries 512x4 bits of RAM inside the mapper, so GBMBCInit sets sramSize to
0x100 -- smaller than the GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM bank size GBMBCSwitchSramBank is
written around. Every path through that function's bounds check then fails and
it returns without assigning sramBank.

That is reachable and fatal. GBLoadSave calls GBSramDeinit, which frees the SRAM
and NULLs gb->memory.sram but leaves sramBank pointing into the freed mapping,
then calls GBMBCSwitchSramBank to re-point it. With the early return nothing
does -- GBMBCReset ran before the save was loaded -- and the next cartridge RAM
read dereferences freed memory. MBC2 is the mapper that shows it because
_GBMBC2Read indexes sramBank directly (directSramAccess is false), so a cart
that reads its save shortly after boot faults reproducibly: on the Miyoo Mini
Flip, SIGSEGV in _GBMBC2Read via GBLoad8 about 0.9 s after the first frame,
every time, on Kirby's Pinball Land DX (header type 0x06, MBC2+BATTERY, RAM
size byte 0x00 as the spec requires for MBC2).

MBC7 (0x100) and TAMA5 (0x20) are set up the same way and took the same early
return; their read handlers go to mapper registers rather than sramBank, so
they did not fault, but their bank pointer was equally wrong.

One deliberate difference from upstream's version: this also zeroes bankStart
in the sub-bank case. Upstream sets bank = 0 and leaves bankStart at
bank * GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM, which indexes past a 256-byte allocation if it is
ever called with a non-zero bank. Nothing calls it that way today -- MBC2's
RAM is not banked and sramCurrentBank stays 0 -- so this is belt and braces,
and it is why the patch will not apply cleanly once libretro rebases.

Applies to libretro/mgba at e31759b24e7a4e3899285ff720d7b573ac328ae7.

diff --git a/src/gb/mbc.c b/src/gb/mbc.c
index 8f41902..cca853c 100644
--- a/src/gb/mbc.c
+++ b/src/gb/mbc.c
@@ -267,15 +267,28 @@ static enum GBMemoryBankControllerType _detectUnlMBC(const uint8_t* mem, size_t
 void GBMBCSwitchSramBank(struct GB* gb, int bank) {
 	size_t bankStart = bank * GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM;
 	if (bankStart + GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM > gb->sramSize) {
-		mLOG(GB_MBC, GAME_ERROR, "Attempting to switch to an invalid RAM bank: %0X", bank);
 		if (gb->sramSize == 0) {
 			return;
 		}
-		bankStart &= toPow2(gb->sramSize) - 1;
-		if (bankStart + GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM > gb->sramSize) {
-			return;
+		if (gb->sramSize < GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM) {
+			// Some mappers carry less SRAM than one 8 KB bank: MBC2's 512x4
+			// bits (0x100), MBC7 (0x100) and TAMA5 (0x20). Bank 0 is the only
+			// bank such a cart has. Returning instead, as this did, leaves
+			// sramBank unassigned -- and GBLoadSave calls GBSramDeinit, which
+			// frees the SRAM and leaves sramBank pointing into the freed
+			// mapping, before calling us to re-point it. The next cartridge
+			// RAM read then uses freed memory.
+			bank = 0;
+			bankStart = 0;
+		} else {
+			mLOG(GB_MBC, GAME_ERROR, "Attempting to switch to an invalid RAM bank: %0X", bank);
+			bankStart &= toPow2(gb->sramSize) - 1;
+			if (bankStart + GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM > gb->sramSize) {
+				bank = 0;
+			} else {
+				bank = bankStart / GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM;
+			}
 		}
-		bank = bankStart / GB_SIZE_EXTERNAL_RAM;
 	}
 	gb->memory.sramBank = &gb->memory.sram[bankStart];
 	gb->memory.sramCurrentBank = bank;
